                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "k47":</h3><p><b>K47</b> is the name of a condenser microphone capsule, created by Neumann around 1960, possibly <a href="http://www.neumann.com/forums/view.php?site=neumann&amp;bn=neumann_archive&amp;key=1077849858">as early as 1958</a>, to replace the M7 (whose diaphragm was made of PVC plastic and subject to relatively rapid deterioration).</p><p>The K47 is a large-diaphragm capsule with a Mylar (polyester) diaphragm. The Mylar film is extremely thin, just 6 microns, and is coated with gold. An electrical lead is mounted to the center; this construction is termed &#8220;center-terminated.&#8221;</p><p>A single brass &#8220;backplate,&#8221; outside diameter 34mm, is fitted with two Mylar diaphragms, one on each side. The diaphragms are attached via a screwed-down tensioning ring. This is a contrast to the M7, whose diaphragms were glued to the backplate.</p><p>By combining the electrical signals produced by the two diaphragms, multiple pickup patterns can be produced. </p><p>The use of a single backplate presented tuning challenges. If the opposing diaphragms were not tensioned equally, the capsule&#8217;s figure-of-8 and omnidirectional patterns would have been adversely affected. Neumann&#8217;s next major capsule design, the K67, utilized a split backplate, allowing each capsule half to be tuned independently, then joined into matched pairs.</p><p>Historically, Neumann maintained multiple quality designations, indicating the relative parity of the front and back half of each capsule.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Martin Schneider, 2007</cite></p><p>In earlier days we made a distinction, K49 was the best possibly matched capsules front/rear; K47 was the second best classification; and for the U47fet we needed only one side to be perfect, so that capsule was called K47fet.</p><p>Now, our tolerances in the production of this capsule are so small that we do not make this distinction anymore, and both K47 and K49 are now called K47/49, and are used for all U47, U48, M49, M249, M149Tube.</p></blockquote><p>The K47 capsule, albeit with a plastic tensioning ring rather than the original brass, is still manufactured by Neumann (Sennheiser) today.</p><p>One of the ways a K47 can be visually distinguished is the pattern of holes in the backplate. The outer ring of holes forms a circle around the center pin, with each hole equidistant from the center. Contrast this with a K67, whose backplate holes form tangents to the circumference.<br /><a href="/images/mic_extras/neumann/k47_capsule.jpg" rel="shadowbox"><img src="/images/mic_extras/neumann/k47_capsule_sm.jpg" width="200" alt="Neumann K-47 capsule" /></a></p><blockquote cite="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/mv/msg/30614/0/48/0/#msg_num_6"><p><cite>Klaus Heyne</cite></p><p>K47/49 from early 1959 to 1963/4 used diaphragm rings made of brass. Then came fibreboard (medium to dark brown) then ivory colored plastic, then, through now, white plastic.</p></blockquote><p>The K47/K49 capsule does not produce a pure Cardioid pickup pattern; it tends to become <a href="http://recforums.prosoundweb.com/index.php/mv/msg/32374/0/32/0/">more directional at higher frequencies</a>.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Martin Schneider</cite></p><p>The standard K47/49, just like the M7 is an in-between capsule. Cardioid somewhere below 1kHz, super/hypercardioid the higher you go. That&#8217;s the way the capsule came out of the design process in 1935. And that&#8217;s why the manual for U47fet said &#8216;supercardioid&#8217;.</p></blockquote><p></p>
